ref 17592186045417] [17592186198510 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198510 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198510 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198510 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198510 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198510 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198510 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198510 #edb/ref 1045 "Constrained Optimization of Quadratic Forms: Determining Where Extrema are Attained" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198510 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186199393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198510 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198510 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198510 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f3742955-3684-5655-938c-85f92fc58b37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198524 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198524 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198524 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198524 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198524 #edb/ref 1045 "Orthogonal Linear Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198524 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186199401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198524 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198524 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198524 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5f56a4f9-bc1e-5b83-af00-1c3cf76a9018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198562 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186414673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198562 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198562 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198562 #edb/ref 1071 "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $3.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198562 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48e09a9b-efe0-5a6d-96f4-c82dbcd33806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198562 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186198971 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186373019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198971 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39418" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198971 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198971 #edb/ref 1071 "Change the order of integration in the integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nso that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $x,$ and then $z.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198971 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db074f68-4935-5a60-b671-6e46f6804cee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198971 #edb/ref 1074 "We wish to find limits of integration ${u}_{1}(x,z),$ ${u}_{2}(x,z),$ ${v}_{1}(z),$ ${v}_{2}(z),$ $a,$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the region of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region. Since it is a type II region, we first sketch the projection onto the $yz$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nE=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/e3/e32a019900b77c0bb84b4599b8899e3d0d32ae07d9674da126db34394b97ea24.png)\n\nThen, the region $R$ is shown in the diagram below, where $0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{tan}⁡y.$\n\n![](images/af/af629672ff2fd31eeae585ad4941fb8682941444cd34383392fb0a6bec24396e.png)\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $R$ as a type III region and its projection $D$ onto the $xz$-plane as a type II *plane* region.\n\nTo visualize this, imagine firing an arrow through the region parallel to the $y$-axis and in the direction of increasing $y,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/04/04dc917237ab002263e35fb4a84c264fef240fefaa456c5249d6e24d0c8ebe28.png)\n\nThe arrow enters the region through the left boundary and leaves through the right boundary:\n\n- The left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x,z),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.\n$$\n\n- The right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the type III representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,\\,\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b2/b2a6fc02441bb6013b6f54d49ceefafc1468b291ae4149847290135bbf2d2e56.png)\n\nWe now consider the projection $D$ as a type II plane region in the $xz$-plane with the top curve given by \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1.\n$$\n\n![](images/b9/b942dbfba8276421620df6258c9951c2b91a5d2dea18c84260c90b6cbe6f760b.png)\n\nHence, we have that\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration so that it is integrated first with respect to $y,$ then $x,$ then $z,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x}^{\\pi /4}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199270 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199270 #edb/ref 1061 43054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199270 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd96d187-aeb9-5dea-b110-23ee93857c07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199270 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199271 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199271 #edb/ref 1061 43193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199271 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2ecadc5e-e56f-5cb0-8a78-a7a91da258bd" #edb/ref 17592186